-- title:
--   The Hecke algebra of Sₙ₊₂(Γ₀(N)) is ℤ-finite
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number, let $n$ be a natural number, and let $S$ be a set of natural numbers such that every prime $q$ dividing $N$ belongs to $S$. Consider the space $S_{n+2}(\Gamma_0(N))$ of cusp forms of weight $(n:\mathbb{Z})+2$ for $\Gamma_0(N)$, and inside $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}} S_{n+2}(\Gamma_0(N))$ the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N ((n : ℤ) + 2) S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), defined as the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra generated by the set consisting of the operators `heckeTLin` attached to the primes $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$, together with the operators `heckeULin` attached to the primes $q$ with $q \mid N$ and $q \notin S$. The assertion is that this subalgebra is finitely generated as a module over $\mathbb{Z}$. Note that the hypothesis on $S$ makes the second family of generators empty, so the algebra in question is the one generated by the Hecke operators $T_\ell$ at the primes $\ell$ outside $S$ that do not divide $N$; in particular the $U_q$ for $q \mid N$ are excluded, and the statement concerns only the weights of the shape $n+2$ with $n \ge 0$.
--
--   This is the standard finiteness of the anemic Hecke algebra acting on cusp forms of weight $n+2$ for $\Gamma_0(N)$: it carries an integral structure coming from parabolic cohomology with $\operatorname{Sym}^n$ coefficients rather than from $q$-expansions. It is used to produce integral and mod $p$ eigenclasses in parabolic cohomology attached to ideals of the Hecke algebra, via [`HeckeEis.exists_coeffH1par_int_modp_eigenclass_of_ideal_heckeAlgebra`](thm.html#HeckeEis.exists_coeffH1par_int_modp_eigenclass_of_ideal_heckeAlgebra).
